<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alpes Maritimae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14 BCE–5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Maritime Alps province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alpes Maritimae was a small Roman province in the Maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, organised under Augustus after the Alpine peoples were subdued, first under a prefect from 14 BCE. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cemenelum">Cemenelum</a>, now Cimiez in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, and in late antiquity Embrun. It guarded the coastal and mountain routes between Italy and southern Gaul, and its name survives in the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Alpes-Maritimes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alpes-maritimae.jpg" alt="Alpes Maritimae" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Alpes_Maritimae_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
